WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:05.370
The ending is one of the most important parts
of any movie, so it's no surprise that directors

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.410
have often wavered back and forth on what
tone to end their films on.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:12.990
Even movies that would eventually become classics
almost had significantly different endings

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:14.730
that were originally planned.

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:19.930
From boxing dramas to Mafia epics, here are
a few movies that almost had much happier

00:00:19.930 --> 00:00:21.020
endings.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:22.020
Rocky Balboa

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:27.860
Whether he wins by knockout or loses by split
decision, Rocky Balboa almost always walks

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.770
away from the ring with a smile on his face.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.079
As the man puts it himself:

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:34.810
"It ain't about how hard ya hit.

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:38.700
It's about how hard you can get it and keep
moving forward.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.739
How much you can take and keep moving forward."

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:47.260
At the end of Rocky Balboa, the Italian Stallion
loses in a split decision, but wins the respect

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:49.060
of everyone watching the fight.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.870
However, there's an alternate ending in which
the brawler bests his opponent, walking away

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.780
with a razor-thin decision.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:04.880
"Heavyweight champion, Roockyyyyyyyyy Balboa!"

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.120
Is it happier?

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.810
Yeah, but it isn't Rocky.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:11.430
My Best Friend's Wedding

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:16.380
After a series of mishaps and hijinks, Julia
Roberts eventually gives up on romancing her

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.470
best friend and spends the last moments of
the movie dancing with another friend instead.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:25.990
In the original ending of My Best Friend's
Wedding, Roberts finds a potential new boyfriend

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:30.030
at her friend's wedding, but test audiences
were confused by the sudden appearance of

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:30.700
a new beau.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:32.120
"Hi.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:35.700
I'm Andy Connelly.

00:01:36.020 --> 00:01:37.860
We haven't met."

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:43.090
Test audiences liked Rupert Everett just way
too much, so he was able to make his triumphant

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.090
return in the finale.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:46.479
"You're going to humiliate me, aren't you?"

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:47.380
"Only if I can."

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:49.500
"Just one thing, stay away from-"

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:51.360
Easy Rider

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.400
This seminal biker flick follows a bunch of
laid back hippies on a cross-country road

00:01:55.400 --> 00:01:58.829
trip right up until an incredibly violent
ending.

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:02.810
Right before the credits roll, we see the
bikers lying dead on the road, with their

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.729
motorcycles in flames.

00:02:04.729 --> 00:02:09.610
While director Dennis Hopper originally wanted
the bikers to make it out alive, writer Terry

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:14.930
Southern told The Paris Review that he'd convinced
the director that the dark ending was, quote,

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.240
"more or less mandatory" for Easy Rider.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:19.160
"You got a helmet?"

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:25.160
"Oh ho ho, I've got a helmet."

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:26.280
Why?

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:31.340
Southern wanted to write, quote, "an indictment
of blue-collar America, the people [he] thought

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:33.770
were responsible for the Vietnam War."

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:35.380
Message received.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.800
Rogue One: A Star Wars Story

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.510
A prequel to A New Hope, Rogue One follows
a group of resistance fighters who are able

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:47.270
to steal the plans for the Death Star by sacrificing
themselves in the process.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:52.030
However, in one of the early drafts of the
screenplay, the ragtag group actually makes

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.610
it out alive.

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:55.989
Director Gareth Edwards explained to the Empire
podcast,

00:02:55.989 --> 00:03:00.970
"It was just assumed by us that we couldn't
[kill the cast], and they're not gonna let

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:01.970
us do that."

00:03:01.970 --> 00:03:06.120
As it turned out, they actually could, and
the result is one of the best sequences in

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.160
Star Wars history.

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:09.260
Chinatown

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.080
Working for a mysterious woman named Evelyn
Mulwray, Jake Gittes uncovers a massive fraud

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.130
operation in Los Angeles.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:23.730
Worse, he that the father of Evelyn's child
is actually Noah Cross, Evelyn's twisted father.

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:28.430
It's pretty grim stuff, all capped off by
a showdown in Chinatown where Evelyn is killed

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:31.900
while Cross steals his daughter-granddaughter.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.409
Gittes stands in the middle of the street,
defeated, as a colleague tells him:

00:03:36.409 --> 00:03:37.870
"Forget it, Jake.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:38.860
It's Chinatown."

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:46.760
However, Robert Towne's original screenplay
would have had Gittes and Mulwray murder Cross,

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.519
allowing Evelyn and her daughter to finally
escape his grasp.

00:03:50.519 --> 00:03:56.159
Director Roman Polanski, in a very dark place
himself due to the recent murder of his wife,

00:03:56.159 --> 00:04:01.100
rewrote an ending where evil triumphs over
good, and added the famous line to create

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:03.670
a grim, unforgettable ending.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:04.769
The Thing

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:09.489
When a shape-shifting alien attacks an Antarctic
research station, the crew must band together

00:04:09.489 --> 00:04:13.670
to fight this otherworldly foe that can look
like anyone and anything.

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.880
Who's human, and who's a thing?

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.750
That's the ultimate question posed by The
Thing's downer of a finale.

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:24.349
The film ends with two men sharing a bottle
of whiskey, freezing to death, and waiting

00:04:24.349 --> 00:04:26.569
to see who's really who.

00:04:26.569 --> 00:04:30.960
Editor Todd Ramsay was so worried about the
ending that he persuaded Carpenter to film

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:33.870
a backup, just in case they had to make a
change later on.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.210
According to Ramsay:

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:38.389
"Just shoot an ending where we see that he
survives."

00:04:38.389 --> 00:04:44.979
"Kurt has been rescued and is sitting in a
small office structure and he's just had a

00:04:44.979 --> 00:04:47.469
blood test so we know that he's not infected."

00:04:47.469 --> 00:04:53.599
Fortunately, Carpenter stuck to his guns and
The Thing is now recognized as a classic,

00:04:53.599 --> 00:04:56.699
largely thanks to its ultra-ambiguous ending.

00:04:56.699 --> 00:04:57.939
The Godfather

00:04:57.939 --> 00:05:03.520
After rising to the top of the Mafia mountain,
Michael Corleone has truly lost his way, going

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:09.000
so far as to murder his own brother-in-law,
deny it to his wife, and close himself off

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:12.389
from her in the most tragic door slam in cinematic
history.

00:05:12.389 --> 00:05:16.990
Believe it or not, director Francis Ford Coppola
actually shot an alternate ending.

00:05:16.990 --> 00:05:21.449
In this deleted scene, we see Kay visiting
a church and lighting candles for Michael's

00:05:21.449 --> 00:05:25.699
soul, hoping there might be a chance her husband
can be redeemed.

00:05:25.699 --> 00:05:30.719
But even though the ending was closer to Mario
Puzo's novel, Coppola realized that he'd already

00:05:30.719 --> 00:05:33.639
shot the perfect ending and left it there.

00:05:33.639 --> 00:05:35.210
"Don't be afraid Carlo.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:39.569
Come on, you think I'd make my sister a widow?"

00:05:39.569 --> 00:05:41.399
The Fly

00:05:41.399 --> 00:05:47.229
David Cronenberg's The Fly follows Seth Brundle,
a brilliant scientist who accidentally merges

00:05:47.229 --> 00:05:48.899
his body with a fly.

00:05:48.899 --> 00:05:54.279
The movie ends with Brundle completely mutated,
finally killed by his pregnant girlfriend

00:05:54.279 --> 00:05:56.259
in a moment of tragic horror.

00:05:56.259 --> 00:06:01.300
However, Cronenberg actually considered going
with several other endings; one possible ending

00:06:01.300 --> 00:06:06.469
has Veronica drifting off to sleep and dreaming
that her future child will hatch from a chrysalis

00:06:06.469 --> 00:06:09.270
and fly away with butterfly wings.

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:14.330
Thankfully, Cronenberg realized that after
the tragic end of Brundle, audiences wouldn't

00:06:14.330 --> 00:06:19.909
be happy walking away with visions of butterfly
children, and stuck to his original ending.

00:06:19.909 --> 00:06:21.080
"Oh...

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.419
That's disgusting."

00:06:23.419 --> 00:06:24.419
Thanks for watching!

00:06:24.419 --> 00:06:26.929
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:26.929 --> 00:06:29.079
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

